---
description: Guidelines for authoring effective Cursor rules using MDC format and XML tags, based on best practices.
globs:
alwaysApply: false
---
<rule_writing_guidelines>
  <objective>
  Create Cursor rules (.mdc files) that are maximally effective. Rules should be clearly structured using MDC format and descriptive XML tags, contain specific and actionable instructions, include illustrative examples, and clearly state their purpose to ensure LLMs can understand and follow them correctly.
  </objective>

  <principles>
    <principle name="mdc_format">
      <summary>Use Correct MDC Format</summary>
      <details>
      - Start EVERY rule file with a `---` block for metadata.
      - Include a clear `description:` explaining the rule's purpose.
      - Use `#` comments within the header for human-readable explanations.
      - Set `alwaysApply: true` for general style or behavioral rules that should always be active. Use file/language filters or `alwaysApply: false` (requiring explicit @mention) for more specific rules.
      </details>
    </principle>

    <principle name="xml_tags">
      <summary>Structure with XML Tags (Anthropic Best Practice)</summary>
      <details>
      - Wrap the main content of the rule body in a descriptive top-level XML tag (e.g., `<commenting_guidelines>`, `<rule_writing_guidelines>`).
      - Use specific XML tags to structure different parts of the rule, such as:
        - `<objective>`: State the overall goal of the rule.
        - `<instructions>`: Provide general directions.
        - `<do>` / `<dont>`: List specific positive/negative constraints.
        - `<guideline>` / `<principle>`: Define specific standards or concepts.
        - `<example>` / `<good_example>` / `<bad_example>`: Provide concrete illustrations.
        - `<context>`: Supply necessary background information.
      - Use tag names that are descriptive and relevant to the content they enclose.
      - Be consistent with tag usage within a rule.
      - Nest tags appropriately for hierarchical information (`<outer><inner>...</inner></outer>`).
      - Reference the content within tags when explaining (e.g., "Follow the steps in `<instructions>`").
      </details>
      <reference>See: https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/use-xml-tags</reference>
    </principle>

    <principle name="clarity_and_specificity">
      <summary>Be Clear, Direct, and Specific</summary>
      <details>
      - Avoid ambiguity. Instructions should be explicit and leave little room for interpretation.
      - Define terms or concepts if they might be unclear.
      - Use strong, direct language (e.g., "MUST", "DO NOT", "ALWAYS", "NEVER").
      - Break down complex rules into smaller, manageable points or sub-tags.
      </details>
    </principle>

    <principle name="actionability">
      <summary>Ensure Rules are Actionable</summary>
      <details>
      - Rules should guide concrete actions or enforce specific coding patterns/styles.
      - Focus on *how* the AI should behave or *what* output it should produce/avoid.
      - Think in terms of programmable outcomes (Huntley's concept).
      </details>
      <reference>Inspired by: https://ghuntley.com/stdlib/</reference>
    </principle>

    <principle name="examples">
      <summary>Provide Illustrative Examples</summary>
      <details>
      - Use examples extensively to demonstrate correct and incorrect application of the rule.
      - Place examples within clear `<example>`, `<good_example>`, or `<bad_example>` tags.
      - Make examples concise and directly relevant to the point they illustrate.
      </details>
    </principle>

    <principle name="stdlib_mindset">
      <summary>Adopt a Standard Library Mindset (Huntley)</summary>
      <details>
      - Treat rules as reusable components in a "standard library" for interacting with the AI.
      - Aim to create rules that solve classes of problems or enforce consistent standards across the project.
      - Refine rules based on observed AI behavior (if a rule isn't followed, improve its clarity or specificity).
      </details>
      <reference>See: https://ghuntley.com/stdlib/</reference>
    </principle>
  </principles>

  <example_rule_structure title="Example: Structure of a Good Rule (like commenting-style.mdc)">
    ```mdc
    ---
    # Header comment explaining purpose
    description: Clear description of the rule's goal.
    alwaysApply: true # Or false, or file filters
    # More header comments
    ---

    <top_level_descriptive_tag> <!-- e.g., <commenting_guidelines> -->

      <objective>
      State the primary goal of this rule clearly.
      </objective>

      <do> <!-- Or <instructions>, <guidelines>, etc. -->
      - Specific positive action/requirement 1.
      - Specific positive action/requirement 2.
      </do>

      <dont>
      - Specific negative constraint 1.
      - Specific negative constraint 2.
      </dont>

      <examples>
        <good_example title="Demonstrates correct behavior">
          // Code or explanation
        </good_example>
        <bad_example title="Demonstrates incorrect behavior">
          // Code or explanation
        </bad_example>
      </examples>

      <!-- Other relevant sections using descriptive tags -->

    </top_level_descriptive_tag>
    ```
  </example_rule_structure>

</rule_writing_guidelines>
